For a source unit with formulas, determine their semantic correspondence within its allocated text. Each formula occurrence has a distinct source key even if two formulas look identical. formula_context gives the original surrounding words for each identity; use this evidence to distinguish repeated symbols by their semantic role. The translated_math table assigns tN IDs to the actual mathematical occurrences AFTER translation; these IDs are only for you, never for the artist. For a delimited expression, return {"key": "s1", "target": "t7"}, or {"keys": ["s1", "s2"], "target": "t7"} for a combined expression. Choose by semantic correspondence, not source/target order. Repeated identical expressions have different tN identities. This selects the translated occurrence, NOT necessarily an entire source-owned replacement. A source formula node may cover only part of it: ordinary source prose, numbers or punctuation may have moved INSIDE the artist's mathematical delimiters. Those residual characters must survive. When an occurrence includes content outside the source nodes, add parts, a complete ordered partition of its EXACT contents WITHOUT the outer math delimiters. Each part is {"keys": ["s1"], "text": "exact source-owned translated subspan"} or {"text": "exact residual to preserve"}. Concatenating all part texts must reproduce the whole delimiter contents exactly, including spaces. Each selected source key must appear exactly once in the parts. When parts is present, its keys are the complete source identity assignment; no duplicate outer key/keys field is needed. For one target combining s1 and s2, assign s1 and s2 in its parts, rather than creating overlapping target entries. Outer key/keys shorthand may name some or all of those same identities, but must not claim any additional one. For example, source s1 is \(0<x<\) followed by ordinary prose 1, and the artist writes \(0<x<1\): return {"key":"s1", "target":"t7", "parts":[{"keys":["s1"],"text":"0<x<"},{"text":"